course: language
lesson: 27
title: Homophones: words that sound the same
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Homophones are words that sound the same but have different meanings and usually different spellings, like "knight" and "night", or "sea" and "see".
explain: Ava reads "The knight rode at night" and frowns: out loud the two words are twins, but on paper they wear different clothes, and the meaning tells you which one to write.
explain: Many homophones once sounded different, and spelling still remembers the old sounds; the k in "knight" was once said out loud.
explain: Homophones also depend on accent: a pair that sounds the same in New York may sound different in London or Glasgow, and both accents are fine.
explain: The trick for spelling is to think about meaning first, and to use little memory hooks, like "you hear with your ear, so heard has ear in it".
fact: Homophones are words with the same sound but a different meaning and often a different spelling: knight and night, sea and see, son and sun, write and right.
fact: "Flour" and "flower" were once the same word; "flower" also meant the best part, and flour was the finest part of the ground grain; the spellings split only a few hundred years ago.
fact: "Bear" means put up with, carry, or the animal; "bare" means uncovered or empty; "I can't bear it" means I can't stand it.
fact: For most English speakers "which" and "witch" sound the same, but many speakers in Scotland and Ireland say "wh" with a breathy sound, so the two are different.
fact: "Heard" is the past of hear; "herd" is a group of animals like cows; "heard" has "ear" in it.
fact: "Write" (put down words), "right" (correct, or not left), "rite" (a ceremony) and "wright" (a maker, as in playwright) all sound the same.
fact: In many American accents "Mary", "marry" and "merry" sound the same; in many British accents they are three different sounds.
fact: "Buy" means pay for, "by" means near or through, and "bye" means goodbye.
fact: "Prey" is an animal hunted for food; "pray" means speak to God or a god.
fact: "Stationery" with an e means paper, pens and envelopes ("e for envelopes"); "stationary" with an a means standing still.
exercise: Ava reads "The knight rode at night." What are "knight" and "night"? | Homophones: same sound, different meaning and spelling | The same word spelled two ways, one old and one modern | Opposites | A spelling mistake made by the printer of the book
exercise: Which pair are homophones? | Sea and see | Cat and dog | Read and book | Sun and moon
exercise: Sam asks if "flour" and "flower" are related. What's true? | Yes, they were once the same word | No, it's pure coincidence | Yes, flour is made from flowers | No, one is French and one is Latin
exercise: Lily writes "I can't bare it!" Which word is correct? | Bear | Bare | Bair | Beer
exercise: What does "bare" mean? | Uncovered or empty | Put up with | A big furry animal | Carry something heavy
exercise: Harvey's Scottish gran says "which" and "witch" sound different. Is she right? | Yes, in her accent "wh" has a breathy sound | No, they sound the same for everyone in the world | No, she is saying it wrong and should be corrected | Yes, but only when she sings
exercise: Jade writes "I herd a sound." What should it be? | Heard | Hurd | Herd | Hered
exercise: What is a herd? | A group of animals like cows | The past of hear | A shepherd's stick | A kind of bird
exercise: Rory must "write the right rite". What is a rite? | A ceremony | A pen | The opposite of left | A maker of things
exercise: In "playwright", what does "wright" mean? | Maker | Writer of letters | Correct | Ceremony
exercise: A New York boy says "Mary", "marry" and "merry" the same, and his London friend says them differently. Who is right? | Both, homophones depend on accent | Only the London friend | Only the New York boy | Neither, they should all rhyme with "hurry"
exercise: Ellie writes "I'm going to by a new pen." Which word should it be? | Buy | Bye | By | Bie
exercise: Ben writes "The dog ate its pray." Which word should it be? | Prey | Pray | Prei | Prae
exercise: Mia's mum buys paper and envelopes. How does she spell what she buys? | Stationery | Stationary | Stashionery | Stationnary
exercise: A bus that is standing still is what? | Stationary | Stationery | Stationed | Statuesque
test: Your mate goes "homophones are just the same word, innit, spell 'em how u like." What's true? | They have different meanings, so spelling matters | He's right, any spelling works as long as it sounds right | Only teachers care about meaning | They're only found in very old books
test: Your little cousin writes "the herd of cows" and her friend says it should be "heard". Who's right? | Your cousin, a herd is a group of animals | The friend, it's always spelled "heard" whatever it means | Both spellings mean animals | Neither, it's "hurd"
test: Ivy's mate from Edinburgh says "aye, which and witch are no the same tae me." ("Aye" means yes, "no" here means not, "tae" means to.) What's true? | Many Scots really say them differently | She's putting on an act to sound more Scottish | Scottish accents are wrong | They sound the same everywhere
test: What would happen if a shop sign said "Stationary for sale"? | It would say "standing still for sale", a funny mistake | Nothing, both spellings are fine | It would mean pens are free | It would mean the shop is closed
test: A Gen Alpha kid writes "the lion hunted its pray, no cap." ("No cap" means for real.) What's the spelling mistake? | It should be "prey", a hunted animal | It should be "pree" | "Its" should be "it's", with an apostrophe | Nothing, "pray" is right
test: Your dad says "bear and bare are one word with two spellings." What's true? | They are different words with different meanings | He's right, they're one word | "Bare" is the American spelling of the British "bear" | "Bear" is only the animal
test: In a cartoon a knight rides at night, and the speech bubble says "Good nite, Sir Night!" What's wrong? | The knight's title should be spelled "Knight" | Nothing, it's all spelled correctly | Knights can't ride at night | "Sir" should be "Sur"
test: Your Aussie mate texts "gonna by heaps of lollies this arvo." ("Arvo" means afternoon, "lollies" means sweets.) What word should be "buy"? | By | Heaps | Gonna | Lollies
test: Tom says there can only be two homophones for one sound. Which shows he's wrong? | Write, right, rite and wright | Cat and hat | Knight and day | Sun and moon
test: Which memory hook helps with "heard"? | You hear with your ear, and heard has "ear" in it | Heard rhymes with bird, and birds sing so you hear them | Herd has an "h" for hearing | Heard has "earth" in it
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises homophones? | Words identical in pronunciation but differing in meaning and frequently in spelling | Words identical in spelling and meaning | Words of opposite meaning | Words borrowed unaltered from French
exam: Identify the correct form in the sentence "The lion seized its ___." | Prey | Pray | Pree | Preigh
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the words "which" and "witch". | They are pronounced identically by most speakers but distinctly by many in Scotland and Ireland | They are pronounced identically in every accent of English, without any exception whatsoever | They are pronounced distinctly in every accent of English | The distinction is regarded as an error
exam: Ascertain which word denotes writing materials such as paper and envelopes. | Stationery | Stationary | Stationed | Statutory
